{
  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "The quantitative claim in Level 1’s “What‑if” – “with no vaccination, more than 5 more people are infectious at year 20 than in this world” – is not supported by the paper. The paper never provides a specific numeric difference for the infectious compartment at year 20; it only discusses qualitatively that vaccination reduces spread.",
      "kind": "overstated"
    ,
    "level": 1
    ,
    "severity": "advice"
    ,
    "suggestion": "Replace the precise “> 5 more infectious at year 20” statement with a qualitative description such as “without vaccination the number of infectious individuals is higher at year 20”."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 2 attributes a quotation to the paper – “The paper shows that vaccinating every newborn would eliminate tuberculosis within ten years” – that does not appear anywhere in the source text (provenance note). Moreover, the quoted statement concerns vaccination, not the treatment‑rate manipulation that the level is meant to explore.",
      "kind": "unsupported",
      "level": 2,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Remove the mis‑attributed quotation and replace it with a statement that actually appears in the paper, e.g., “The paper shows that if the effective reproduction number ℛᵥ < 1, the disease‑free equilibrium is locally asymptotically stable.”"
    },
    {
      "finding": "The “What‑if” claim in Level 3 – “the epidemic doubles in size and the disease can never be controlled” – is far stronger than the measured effect (only 6 more exposed individuals at year 10). The paper does not claim an uncontrolled epidemic under a doubled progression rate.",
      "kind": "overstated",
      "level": 3,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Re‑phrase the claim to match the measured outcome, e.g., “the number of exposed individuals is higher (by about 6 at year 10) when the progression rate is doubled.”"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 4’s effect is purely definitional: setting the recovery‑from‑treatment rate higher simply moves individuals from the treated (T) compartment to the recovered (R) compartment faster, which the model guarantees. This teaches nothing beyond the model’s definitions, and the level also fails its own rule (9 of 20 checks).",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 4,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the current “What‑if” with one that tests a paper finding, e.g., “What‑if: increase the recovery rate α by 50 % and observe how the basic reproduction number ℛ₀ and the endemic equilibrium sizes change?”"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 5 is also definitional: setting the treatment rate δ to zero guarantees that the treated compartment (T) remains empty, which follows directly from the model equations and does not illustrate any of the paper’s substantive results.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 5,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the current “What‑if” with a scenario that probes a paper result, e.g., “What‑if: halve the treatment rate δ and examine the resulting increase in the infected compartment and the change in the effective reproduction number ℛᵥ.”"
    },
    {
      "finding": "The word “infectious” is used ambiguously in several level descriptions (e.g., “watch the infectious” and “more people are infectious”). In the model, “infectious” refers specifically to the I‑compartment, while the treated compartment (T) also contains individuals who can cause disease‑induced death. This can confuse learners about which compartment is being observed.",
      "kind": "unsupported",
      "level": null,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Replace ambiguous uses of “infectious” with the precise compartment name, e.g., “watch the I‑compartment (infectious individuals).”"
    }
  ]
}
